PART 1: WHAT THE PAPER DOES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The paper introduces a "Feature-Guided Multilayer Encoding–Decoding Network" for the semantic segmentation of 3D intraoral dental scans. 

In simple terms:
- It takes a raw, single-mesh 3D digital dental scan (like those from an iTero or 3Shape scanner).
- It uses a highly efficient, lightweight AI model (only 1.54M parameters) to automatically identify and separate every individual tooth and the surrounding gingiva (gums).
- It is specifically trained to handle difficult, real-world clinical anomalies that confuse older models, such as severe crowding, missing teeth, diastemas (gaps), and retained implant roots.
- Because it reduces computational cost (FLOPs) by 44% to 94% compared to standard models, it is light enough to be run via a simple cloud API or directly in a web browser without requiring heavy, expensive GPUs.

